\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{What the two kinds of bound measure}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{In the [controlled input domain](fdip-01DK), Gaussian damping
allows a quartic contribution far from the origin to be small in
the derivative norm. The separate upper curvature bound still
restricts the potential to at most quartic growth. The lower
curvature bound controls the exponential of the fluctuation,
which is the integrand in the [exact marginal map](fdip-01DJ).
These requirements serve different parts of its Gaussian integral.}

\p{For #{V=hW_4(\cdot;s)}, the remainder and quadratic coefficient vanish,
and #{V''(x)=12h(x^2-s)}. Thus this elementary input satisfies
the curvature requirements when #{B\ge12\max(1,s)} and
#{g_*=h}. The output function norm does not bound derivatives,
so a bound in that norm cannot establish membership of the next
potential in the controlled input domain.}
